https://apidocs.lighter.xyz/docs/create-.content:
Onboarding a user takes three steps:
Create the account
– the user deposits; Lighter assigns an account index.
Register an API key
– your app generates a keypair; the user signs
ChangePubKey
once with their wallet.
Trade
– your app signs orders with the API key. No more wallet pop-ups.
1. Create the account
There is no "sign-up" call.
The first deposit creates the account
, allowing you to interact with the exchange without ever needing to touch the front-end. See
Deposits, Transfers and Withdrawals
for all deposit methods.
Direct Ethereum deposit: credited in a few minutes.
Universal Deposit Address
from any supported chain: new accounts are created automatically.
Legacy CCTP route: around 15–20 minutes.
Then look up the master account index with
accountsByL1Address
(or
account
):
TypeScript
const r = await fetch(
  `${BASE_URL}/api/v1/accountsByL1Address?l1_address=$USERADDRESS`,
).then(r => r.json());

if (r.code === 21100) {
  // "account not found" – no deposit credited yet; poll again later
}
const masterAccountIndex = r.sub_accounts[0].index;
You can also read
addressToAccountIndex
(0xabf6a038) on
Lighter's Ethereum contract
.
2. Register an API key
Generating a key doesn't need the user's wallet, but registering it for the account does (
ChangePubKey
with an L1 signature). The TypeScript example below uses the WASM signer from the
TypeScript quickstart
. With the SDKs, see
system_setup.py
(Python) or the
lighter-go README
(Go).
TypeScript
import { lighter, check } from "./signer";

const API_KEY_INDEX = 4; // pick any free index in 4–254 for your app

// a. Generate a keypair locally
const { privateKey, publicKey } = check(lighter.GenerateAPIKey());

// b. Create a signer client bound to the new key (empty URL: all HTTP goes through fetch)
check(lighter.CreateClient("", privateKey, CHAIN_ID, API_KEY_INDEX, accountIndex));

// c. Sign ChangePubKey with the new key
const nonce = await fetch(
  `${BASE_URL}/api/v1/nextNonce?account_index=${accountIndex}&api_key_index=${API_KEY_INDEX}`,
).then(r => r.json()).then(r => r.nonce);

const tx = check(lighter.SignChangePubKey(publicKey, 0, nonce, API_KEY_INDEX, accountIndex));

// d. The user signs the returned message with their Ethereum wallet (personal_sign)
const l1Sig = await walletClient.signMessage({ account: userAddress, message: tx.messageToSign }); // viem
// ethers: const l1Sig = await signer.signMessage(tx.messageToSign);

// e. Attach the L1 signature and send; check that the API accepted it
const txInfo = JSON.parse(tx.txInfo);
txInfo.L1Sig = l1Sig;

const res = await fetch(`${BASE_URL}/api/v1/sendTx`, {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded" },
  body: new URLSearchParams({ tx_type: String(tx.txType), tx_info: JSON.stringify(txInfo) }),
}).then(r => r.json());
if (res.code !== 200) throw new Error(`ChangePubKey rejected: ${res.code} ${res.message}`);

// f. Wait until the key is registered before using it
await waitForApiKey(accountIndex, API_KEY_INDEX, publicKey);

// g. Persist privateKey securely on the user's device (see Key storage below)
code: 200
only means the API accepted the transaction. It still has to execute before the key can sign orders, so poll
apikeys
until it returns your public key:
TypeScript
async function waitForApiKey(accountIndex: number, apiKeyIndex: number, publicKey: string, timeoutMs = 30_000) {
  const want = publicKey.replace(/^0x/, "").toLowerCase(); // the API returns keys without 0x
  const deadline = Date.now() + timeoutMs;
  while (Date.now() < deadline) {
    const r = await fetch(
      `${BASE_URL}/api/v1/apikeys?account_index=${accountIndex}&api_key_index=${apiKeyIndex}`,
    ).then(r => r.json());
    if (r.api_keys?.[0]?.public_key?.toLowerCase() === want) return;
    await new Promise(res => setTimeout(res, 1_000));
  }
  throw new Error("API key not registered in time: check the tx status with GET /api/v1/tx?by=hash&value=<tx_hash>");
}
📘
One app, one key index
Registering a key
replaces
whatever key was previously at that index. Pick a dedicated index for your app so you don't log the user out of another app.
Key storage
Where the key lives
Recommendation
Browser
Encrypt at rest (e.g. WebCrypto with a non-extractable key); never send to your server
Mobile
OS keychain / keystore
Server (on behalf of users)
KMS or secrets manager; one key per user; audit access
If a key is lost, just generate and register a new one at the same index.
Multisig / smart-contract wallets
Smart wallets cannot produce an ECDSA
personal_sign
. Register the key with an on-chain
changePubKey
call instead. See
Multi-signature and smart wallets
.
3. Sub-accounts (optional)
Use sub-accounts to isolate strategies or users' positions. Sign with
SignCreateSubAccount(skipNonce, nonce, apiKeyIndex, accountIndex)
from the master account. Each sub-account needs
its own API key
registered. The number of sub-accounts depends on the tier: see
Account Types
.
4. Upgrade the tier (optional)
Partner fees require the user to be on
Plus
or
Premium/Advanced
. See
Partner Attribution
and
Account Types
.
