Token bridges and gateways
Token bridges build on
L1 and EVM message passing
to connect a token on its native domain to a wrapper on the other domain. A pair of contracts, called
gateways
, manages the tokens on each side. The
escrow gateway
holds the canonical tokens, while the
mint gateway
manages their wrapper.
The protocol provides
default gateway pairs
that handle this message passing and token accounting for L1-native and EVM-native ERC-20 tokens. These pairs can operate before a Core asset exists and are designed so that the existing bridge and wrapper can be linked to that asset later.
Choosing gateways
When launching a token or enabling transfers between L1 and EVM, consider whether you want the bridge and its wrapper to support future Core integration.
The default gateways provide a ready-made bridge with that compatibility built in. If you need more control over the bridge's behavior, you can implement a custom pair. To preserve the option of linking it to a Core asset later, follow the
gateway requirements
and
external wrapper requirements
in Token Linking.
Gateway selection
can connect the pair to the Core asset after registration.
If you do not need future Core integration, you can still use the default gateways or build your own token bridge on the messaging infrastructure. A standalone L1–EVM bridge does not have to implement the interfaces required for Core integration and can define its own transfer rules and interfaces.
These compatibility requirements concern linking an existing bridge and wrapper to Core.
Registering the canonical asset on Core
is a separate process and does not itself require a gateway pair.
Transferring through the default gateways
The procedures below apply to the protocol's
default gateways
. Find their deployed addresses in
Chain Info
. A custom bridge defines its own transfer interface.
The default gateways do not support fee-on-transfer or rebasing tokens. Their accounting assumes that the amount received matches the amount transferred and that escrowed balances do not change independently of transfers.
To find a token's wrapper address, call
computeTokenAddress(canonicalToken)
on the default mint gateway on the wrapper's domain. If the wrapper does not yet exist, it is deployed when the first incoming transfer is successfully delivered. An existing wrapper is reused.
Bridging L1-native tokens
Deposit (L1 to EVM)
Approve
L1EscrowGateway
to spend the canonical L1 token, then call
sendTokens(...)
on that gateway.
